WEBVTT

00:00:00.043 --> 00:00:01.044 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Builder.

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.784 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Blueprint.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.074 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson one.

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:05.355 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Introduction.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.616 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Education only.

00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:09.489 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Not financial advice.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.061 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is general information and

00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:14.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
doesn't consider your objectives,

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
financial situation or needs.

00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:19.179 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto, a digital coin, is high risk;

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:21.614 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you could lose all the money you put in.

00:00:22.134 --> 00:00:24.186 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past performance is not a reliable

00:00:24.186 --> 00:00:25.884 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
indicator of future results.

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:29.326 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The prices are public historical prices

00:00:29.326 --> 00:00:31.505 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at the end of each day, marked Example.

00:00:32.637 --> 00:00:34.536 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No order is sent from this lesson.

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.434 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end of this series you will

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:39.222 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
understand what a grid bot does.

00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:41.072 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is a small program that

00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:42.636 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
places these orders for you.

00:00:43.092 --> 00:00:44.992 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will learn when it can capture

00:00:44.992 --> 00:00:46.556 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces, and where it loses.

00:00:46.803 --> 00:00:48.814 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will know how to plan one, test

00:00:48.814 --> 00:00:50.713 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
it on paper, run it, check it, and

00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:52.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
change the capital you give to bots

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:54.623 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only under written risk rules.

00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:56.590 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is education, not

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:57.957 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a promise of results.

00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:00.087 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price often moves sideways,

00:01:00.087 --> 00:01:01.838 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bouncing between the same top

00:01:01.838 --> 00:01:03.588 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and bottom instead of leaving.

00:01:04.108 --> 00:01:05.818 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid is a ladder of waiting

00:01:05.818 --> 00:01:07.528 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
orders, called limit orders,

00:01:07.528 --> 00:01:09.015 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
built for that bouncing.

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:11.378 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When price runs one way and stays

00:01:11.378 --> 00:01:13.557 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
there, the grid has nothing left to do.

00:01:13.938 --> 00:01:15.691 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
And if price falls through the

00:01:15.691 --> 00:01:17.703 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, coin means the crypto

00:01:17.703 --> 00:01:19.526 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
itself, and you are left holding

00:01:19.526 --> 00:01:21.467 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the coin, and it is worth less.

00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:23.858 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end you will know

00:01:23.858 --> 00:01:25.143 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
what a grid bot places.

00:01:26.272 --> 00:01:28.351 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will see that idea on

00:01:28.351 --> 00:01:30.326 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
one real path from 2023.

00:01:31.526 --> 00:01:33.034 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will be able to say the

00:01:33.034 --> 00:01:34.487 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stop rule in one sentence.

00:01:35.441 --> 00:01:37.287 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When a chart says Example, it

00:01:37.287 --> 00:01:39.132 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
means a real past price used

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:40.978 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
as an example, not a forecast.

00:01:42.028 --> 00:01:43.270 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto is a digital

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.668 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
asset, such as bitcoin.

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:46.863 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You do not need to have bought

00:01:46.863 --> 00:01:48.148 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
any before this lesson.

00:01:48.527 --> 00:01:50.494 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An exchange is the website or app

00:01:50.494 --> 00:01:52.826 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
where you swap Australian dollars for

00:01:52.826 --> 00:01:54.867 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crypto, and crypto back to dollars.

00:01:55.387 --> 00:01:57.063 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course does not name one,

00:01:57.063 --> 00:01:58.739 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it does not send an order.

00:01:59.484 --> 00:02:01.048 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Cash is money you can spend.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.286 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here that means dollars, or a

00:02:03.286 --> 00:02:05.651 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stablecoin, a crypto coin built

00:02:05.651 --> 00:02:07.167 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
to stay at one US dollar.

00:02:07.687 --> 00:02:09.754 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We treat it like cash in this lesson,

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:11.486 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
but it can drop below a dollar.

00:02:11.915 --> 00:02:14.403 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

00:02:15.453 --> 00:02:17.799 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

00:02:18.251 --> 00:02:20.379 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

00:02:20.899 --> 00:02:23.107 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell order swaps coin back to

00:02:23.107 --> 00:02:25.544 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash, and you can only sell coin you

00:02:25.544 --> 00:02:27.446 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
already hold, on a spot account.

00:02:27.966 --> 00:02:29.603 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A spot account is the normal

00:02:29.603 --> 00:02:30.955 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
account on an exchange.

00:02:31.475 --> 00:02:33.073 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You own the coin you buy,

00:02:33.073 --> 00:02:34.414 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and nothing is borrowed.

00:02:35.441 --> 00:02:37.210 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot is software that places

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:38.833 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and refills orders for you.

00:02:39.353 --> 00:02:41.196 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You start with some cash and some

00:02:41.196 --> 00:02:42.918 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, so there is something to

00:02:42.918 --> 00:02:44.650 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy with and something to sell.

00:02:45.011 --> 00:02:47.363 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

00:02:47.363 --> 00:02:49.715 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on lines below the current price and

00:02:49.715 --> 00:02:52.067 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell orders on lines above it, inside

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:53.871 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a top and a bottom you choose.

00:02:54.391 --> 00:02:56.067 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The space between your top and

00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:57.854 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your bottom is called the range.

00:02:57.948 --> 00:03:00.351 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sells need some coin, so a grid

00:03:00.351 --> 00:03:02.301 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
starts with part cash and part

00:03:02.301 --> 00:03:04.548 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, and that is a description,

00:03:04.548 --> 00:03:06.419 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not advice about what to hold.

00:03:07.469 --> 00:03:09.175 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A limit order is an order that

00:03:09.175 --> 00:03:10.740 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waits at a price you choose.

00:03:11.259 --> 00:03:12.823 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not chase the price.

00:03:13.243 --> 00:03:14.807 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Chasing the price would mean

00:03:14.807 --> 00:03:16.259 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
moving the order to follow

00:03:16.259 --> 00:03:17.824 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wherever the price has gone.

00:03:18.673 --> 00:03:20.748 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order fills when the price reaches

00:03:20.748 --> 00:03:22.480 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
its line and the trade happens.

00:03:22.941 --> 00:03:25.148 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Until then, the order is only waiting.

00:03:26.198 --> 00:03:28.153 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

00:03:28.295 --> 00:03:30.641 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:33.207 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A rung is one price where an order waits.

00:03:33.614 --> 00:03:35.793 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The gap is the space between two rungs.

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:38.127 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The ladder is the analogy, and the

00:03:38.127 --> 00:03:40.398 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
chart beside it is the real past path.

00:03:41.448 --> 00:03:42.859 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top rung

00:03:42.859 --> 00:03:43.988 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the bottom rung.

00:03:44.508 --> 00:03:46.631 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the rungs between them.

00:03:46.981 --> 00:03:49.271 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.964 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top line and the

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:53.807 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom line, taken from the path.

00:03:54.286 --> 00:03:55.774 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Hindsight: this range is

00:03:55.774 --> 00:03:57.039 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
drawn after the fact.

00:03:57.559 --> 00:04:00.783 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The chart is bitcoin's real daily

00:04:00.783 --> 00:04:04.353 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

00:04:04.873 --> 00:04:06.459 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Every close in this window

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:08.191 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stayed between those two lines.

00:04:09.091 --> 00:04:11.497 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Six gaps of about 900 dollars each sit

00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:14.058 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
between the bottom line and the top line.

00:04:14.578 --> 00:04:16.805 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

00:04:16.805 --> 00:04:19.032 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the top line minus the bottom

00:04:19.032 --> 00:04:20.517 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
line, divided by six.

00:04:21.037 --> 00:04:22.382 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is one step between

00:04:22.382 --> 00:04:23.951 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines, not the whole range.

00:04:24.471 --> 00:04:26.309 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Seven lines make those six gaps.

00:04:26.829 --> 00:04:28.617 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the prices on the

00:04:28.617 --> 00:04:30.293 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
rungs where limit orders wait.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:32.638 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Buy orders wait on lines

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:33.996 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
below the current price.

00:04:34.516 --> 00:04:36.471 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

00:04:36.952 --> 00:04:39.451 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

00:04:39.451 --> 00:04:42.394 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

00:04:42.914 --> 00:04:44.087 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You choose the range,

00:04:44.087 --> 00:04:45.372 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the gaps and the money.

00:04:45.681 --> 00:04:47.133 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot places each order.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.990 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
After a fill, the bot places the

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.062 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
opposite order one rung up or down.

00:04:52.582 --> 00:04:54.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

00:04:55.147 --> 00:04:57.377 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

00:04:57.897 --> 00:04:59.517 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You still write the stop, and

00:04:59.517 --> 00:05:01.193 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you act when that rule is met.

00:05:01.425 --> 00:05:03.659 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop for you

00:05:03.659 --> 00:05:05.782 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on, and

00:05:05.782 --> 00:05:07.849 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you write which one before you start.

00:05:08.583 --> 00:05:09.924 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a market stall.

00:05:10.356 --> 00:05:12.471 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stallholder buys stock a little

00:05:12.471 --> 00:05:14.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

00:05:14.585 --> 00:05:16.205 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and pays a fee on every sale.

00:05:16.655 --> 00:05:18.224 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Exchanges charge a small

00:05:18.224 --> 00:05:19.271 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fee on each trade.

00:05:19.791 --> 00:05:21.534 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid can do that while price

00:05:21.534 --> 00:05:23.059 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces inside the range.

00:05:23.579 --> 00:05:26.101 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bounce means price moves down and back

00:05:26.101 --> 00:05:28.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

00:05:28.989 --> 00:05:31.226 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One buy at a lower line and the later

00:05:31.226 --> 00:05:33.609 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell of that same coin is one pair.

00:05:34.129 --> 00:05:36.309 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On these daily closes, the July to

00:05:36.309 --> 00:05:38.570 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
October example window finished

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:40.670 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only three pairs, each a buy and a

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.769 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell on lines next to each other.

00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:45.021 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sell line is higher, so the

00:05:45.021 --> 00:05:46.864 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash that comes back is more than

00:05:46.864 --> 00:05:48.820 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the cash that left, before the fee.

00:05:49.113 --> 00:05:50.565 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A fee is what the exchange

00:05:50.565 --> 00:05:52.130 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
charges when an order fills.

00:05:52.311 --> 00:05:54.601 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:05:54.663 --> 00:05:56.171 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the fees are larger than

00:05:56.171 --> 00:05:57.791 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that, the pair keeps nothing.

00:05:57.964 --> 00:05:59.208 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is not a promise.

00:05:59.728 --> 00:06:01.532 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair finishes only if price

00:06:01.532 --> 00:06:03.405 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
does bounce and both orders fill.

00:06:04.455 --> 00:06:05.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The daily close is the

00:06:05.883 --> 00:06:07.112 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
last price of the day.

00:06:07.549 --> 00:06:09.349 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto trades all day, so the

00:06:09.349 --> 00:06:10.757 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
day is cut at a set time.

00:06:11.277 --> 00:06:13.599 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This lesson uses the end of each

00:06:13.599 --> 00:06:15.742 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
UTC day, the world's standard

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:18.243 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
clock, which is 11am Sydney time in

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:20.654 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daylight saving, 10am otherwise.

00:06:21.174 --> 00:06:23.158 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a simple candle that shows

00:06:23.158 --> 00:06:24.985 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the day's high, low and close.

00:06:25.505 --> 00:06:27.435 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Traders call it a candle because

00:06:27.435 --> 00:06:29.364 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the full version has a thick body

00:06:29.364 --> 00:06:31.096 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and a thin wick, like a candle.

00:06:31.528 --> 00:06:33.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we only need the high,

00:06:33.036 --> 00:06:34.290 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the low and the close.

00:06:34.817 --> 00:06:37.996 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This candle is 19 July 2023.

00:06:38.516 --> 00:06:39.745 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high, the low, and

00:06:39.745 --> 00:06:41.089 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the close are labelled.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:44.268 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The example is Bitcoin's public

00:06:44.268 --> 00:06:45.924 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily price in US dollars.

00:06:46.444 --> 00:06:49.621 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

00:06:49.621 --> 00:06:52.545 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023, every one of those days

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:55.087 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closed inside the range.

00:06:55.607 --> 00:06:57.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The close crossed the seven

00:06:57.469 --> 00:06:59.214 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines 28 times in that window.

00:06:59.734 --> 00:07:02.046 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The count is an example, not a forecast.

00:07:02.566 --> 00:07:04.354 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We do not need to read each day.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.962 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The shape is the lesson,

00:07:05.962 --> 00:07:07.376 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it is marked Example.

00:07:07.896 --> 00:07:10.652 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Worked check: 19 July

00:07:10.652 --> 00:07:13.732 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023 to 16 October 2023.

00:07:14.252 --> 00:07:15.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The low is the bottom line.

00:07:16.241 --> 00:07:17.707 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high is the top line.

00:07:18.227 --> 00:07:20.071 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Nothing on this card is an order.

00:07:21.034 --> 00:07:23.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Those 28 crossings, each time the price

00:07:23.904 --> 00:07:26.326 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crosses a line, are not 28 results.

00:07:26.846 --> 00:07:29.245 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A crossing only means the close moved

00:07:29.245 --> 00:07:31.312 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
from one side of a line to the other.

00:07:31.754 --> 00:07:33.465 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A pair is finished only when a

00:07:33.465 --> 00:07:35.319 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy and its later sell have both

00:07:35.319 --> 00:07:37.051 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled, and only after the fee.

00:07:37.551 --> 00:07:39.450 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The price can cross many lines and

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.350 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
still leave you holding coin if it

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:42.970 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
walks out through the bottom.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.628 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You can buy part of a coin.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.210 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we buy one hundredth

00:07:49.251 --> 00:07:50.312 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of a bitcoin, 0.01.

00:07:50.312 --> 00:07:53.575 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
900 dollars times 0.01 bitcoin

00:07:53.575 --> 00:07:56.098 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is 9 dollars before fees.

00:07:56.618 --> 00:07:59.599 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In this example a buy waiting at the

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:02.889 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower of these two lines was reached on

00:08:02.889 --> 00:08:05.870 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
17 August 2023, and the sell one line

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.851 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

00:08:09.371 --> 00:08:11.159 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The card shows the gap, then the

00:08:11.159 --> 00:08:12.723 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
two fees, then what is left.

00:08:13.032 --> 00:08:16.238 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fee is 0.1 percent of the whole

00:08:16.238 --> 00:08:19.113 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
trade, not of the profit: about 27

00:08:19.113 --> 00:08:21.877 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cents on the 267 dollar buy and

00:08:21.877 --> 00:08:24.752 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
about 28 cents on the 276 dollar

00:08:24.752 --> 00:08:27.405 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell, about 54 cents together.

00:08:27.925 --> 00:08:29.750 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is about 8 dollars 46

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.835 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
after fees, for one small pair.

00:08:32.355 --> 00:08:34.254 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It happens only if the price comes

00:08:34.254 --> 00:08:35.930 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
back up through the sell line.

00:08:36.035 --> 00:08:37.766 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the price leaves through the

00:08:37.766 --> 00:08:39.665 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, you still hold the coin,

00:08:39.665 --> 00:08:41.229 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the pair never finishes.

00:08:41.663 --> 00:08:43.451 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This card is not money you made.

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:46.204 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past results don't predict future results.

00:08:53.254 --> 00:08:55.041 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now watch what you hold when the

00:08:55.041 --> 00:08:57.108 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
price leaves the range and stays out.

00:08:57.488 --> 00:08:59.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Above the range the sells have

00:08:59.164 --> 00:09:00.952 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding cash,

00:09:00.952 --> 00:09:02.404 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with nothing left to sell.

00:09:02.726 --> 00:09:04.346 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Below the range the buys have

00:09:04.346 --> 00:09:05.799 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding

00:09:05.799 --> 00:09:07.731 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, with nothing left to buy.

00:09:08.251 --> 00:09:09.643 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is why the way down

00:09:09.643 --> 00:09:11.402 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
matters most: every buy fills,

00:09:11.402 --> 00:09:13.476 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you are left holding coin.

00:09:15.526 --> 00:09:17.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

00:09:18.182 --> 00:09:20.713 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It triggers if a daily close is outside

00:09:20.713 --> 00:09:23.058 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the range, through the top or the bottom,

00:09:23.058 --> 00:09:25.735 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the next daily close is still outside.

00:09:26.255 --> 00:09:27.708 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then the idea is finished.

00:09:28.088 --> 00:09:30.181 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot, which cancels the

00:09:30.181 --> 00:09:32.051 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waiting orders, and do what you

00:09:32.051 --> 00:09:34.455 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

00:09:34.975 --> 00:09:36.957 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot's automatic stop is a price

00:09:36.957 --> 00:09:38.645 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that can fire during the day.

00:09:39.165 --> 00:09:40.864 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course uses the two daily

00:09:40.864 --> 00:09:42.700 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes, unless your written plan

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.488 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
says you turned on a price stop.

00:09:44.919 --> 00:09:46.260 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a teaching rule,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.438 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not a recommendation.

00:09:47.958 --> 00:09:49.578 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is your written rule

00:09:49.578 --> 00:09:51.031 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
for when you stop the bot.

00:09:51.263 --> 00:09:53.486 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On 23 October 2023 the close

00:09:53.486 --> 00:09:55.898 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
finished above the top line, and

00:09:55.898 --> 00:09:58.217 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day was still above it.

00:10:03.267 --> 00:10:04.719 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the top, the sells

00:10:04.719 --> 00:10:06.395 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
have filled and you hold cash.

00:10:06.504 --> 00:10:08.348 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the bottom, the buys have

00:10:08.348 --> 00:10:10.191 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you hold coin that you

00:10:10.191 --> 00:10:12.203 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bought above where the price is now.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:14.382 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That loss can keep growing

00:10:14.382 --> 00:10:15.500 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
until the stop acts.

00:10:15.805 --> 00:10:17.648 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot either way, then

00:10:17.648 --> 00:10:19.213 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you follow the written rule.

00:10:19.322 --> 00:10:21.109 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop

00:10:21.109 --> 00:10:23.009 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on.

00:10:23.281 --> 00:10:24.703 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
EXAMPLE, made up: three

00:10:24.703 --> 00:10:26.283 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buys fill on the way down.

00:10:26.803 --> 00:10:30.102 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth of a bitcoin

00:10:30.102 --> 00:10:33.401 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at 27,630 is 276 dollars 30.

00:10:35.086 --> 00:10:40.620 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 26,730 is 267 dollars 30.

00:10:42.301 --> 00:10:47.483 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 25,830 is 258 dollars 30.

00:10:48.833 --> 00:10:51.473 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Together that is 801 dollars 90.

00:10:52.236 --> 00:10:56.443 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Three buys of 0.01 is 0.03 bitcoin.

00:10:57.067 --> 00:11:00.253 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
At 24,000, that is 720 dollars.

00:11:00.773 --> 00:11:03.040 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A little below the range, at a

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.405 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
made-up price of 24,000, that

00:11:05.405 --> 00:11:07.690 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin is worth 720 dollars.

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:09.836 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You are down 81 dollars 90

00:11:09.836 --> 00:11:11.772 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
before fees, and the loss keeps

00:11:11.772 --> 00:11:13.630 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
growing until your stop acts.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:16.256 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A common mistake is thinking

00:11:16.256 --> 00:11:17.764 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot predicts the price.

00:11:18.553 --> 00:11:19.303 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not.

00:11:19.869 --> 00:11:20.651 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It only waits.

00:11:21.021 --> 00:11:22.529 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Another mistake is starting

00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:23.702 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with no written stop.

00:11:24.117 --> 00:11:25.346 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
There is no promise of

00:11:25.346 --> 00:11:26.631 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
results in this lesson.

00:11:26.869 --> 00:11:28.477 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Numbers marked Example are

00:11:28.477 --> 00:11:30.155 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
teaching numbers, not advice.

00:11:31.205 --> 00:11:33.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper or demo trading is like driving

00:11:33.469 --> 00:11:35.440 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with learner plates, or training

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.485 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wheels: you practise with pretend

00:11:37.485 --> 00:11:39.310 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
money before anything is real.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:41.347 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson 7 shows you how.

00:11:42.404 --> 00:11:43.531 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps one to six.

00:11:44.231 --> 00:11:45.919 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This introduction is step one.

00:11:46.619 --> 00:11:47.681 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then what you need.

00:11:48.311 --> 00:11:49.838 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then exactly what a grid

00:11:49.838 --> 00:11:51.289 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot does, order by order.

00:11:52.289 --> 00:11:53.406 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then market regimes.

00:11:53.982 --> 00:11:56.049 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then choosing grid spacing and range.

00:11:56.688 --> 00:11:58.295 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then entry and exit rules.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.310 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps seven to eleven.

00:12:01.010 --> 00:12:02.094 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then a paper test.

00:12:02.815 --> 00:12:03.876 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then weekly checks.

00:12:04.613 --> 00:12:05.802 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then common mistakes.

00:12:06.502 --> 00:12:08.513 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then scaling up under written rules.

00:12:08.974 --> 00:12:10.364 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then an opt-in lesson on

00:12:10.364 --> 00:12:12.450 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
liquidation and your complete plan.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.400 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The next lesson is a checklist you

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.299 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
write on paper, before any screen.

00:12:18.121 --> 00:12:20.451 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In short: a grid waits, it buys lower and

00:12:20.451 --> 00:12:23.082 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sells higher while price bounces, the fees

00:12:23.082 --> 00:12:25.413 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
come out of what the pair makes, and if a

00:12:25.413 --> 00:12:28.145 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily close leaves and stays out, you stop

00:12:28.145 --> 00:12:30.400 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:12:31.484 --> 00:12:32.350 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Check yourself.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:34.323 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Why does a grid start with

00:12:34.323 --> 00:12:35.780 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
some coin as well as cash?

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:37.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Because the sell orders

00:12:37.585 --> 00:12:39.272 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
need coin you already hold.

00:12:40.472 --> 00:12:43.319 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example pair, why is the net about

00:12:43.319 --> 00:12:46.078 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
8 dollars 46 and not the full 9 dollars?

00:12:46.598 --> 00:12:48.162 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The two fees come out first.

00:12:49.324 --> 00:12:51.391 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price closes above your top line, and

00:12:51.391 --> 00:12:53.580 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day's close is still above it.

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:55.887 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
What do you do, and who does it?

00:12:56.208 --> 00:12:57.996 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot and follow your

00:12:57.996 --> 00:12:59.839 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
written rule, by hand, unless you

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:01.683 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
turned on the bot's stop setting.

00:13:02.739 --> 00:13:03.489 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Your turn.

00:13:03.936 --> 00:13:05.668 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On paper, write the top and the

00:13:05.668 --> 00:13:07.623 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, one gap, and the stop rule.

00:13:08.726 --> 00:13:10.458 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Say what you will do if a daily

00:13:10.458 --> 00:13:12.228 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
close leaves the range and the

00:13:12.228 --> 00:13:13.987 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside.

00:13:14.507 --> 00:13:15.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Pause the video and write

00:13:15.904 --> 00:13:17.524 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your answer before you go on.

00:13:18.557 --> 00:13:20.624 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No real order is sent in this lesson.

00:13:21.111 --> 00:13:23.014 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper practice comes in Lesson

00:13:23.014 --> 00:13:24.624 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
7, and real money only after

00:13:24.624 --> 00:13:26.454 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your written graduation rule.

00:13:26.974 --> 00:13:28.981 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A graduation rule is the written

00:13:28.981 --> 00:13:30.690 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
test you pass on paper first.

00:13:31.223 --> 00:13:32.232 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You draw the idea.

00:13:32.752 --> 00:13:34.546 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a screen appears later, it

00:13:34.546 --> 00:13:36.261 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
should be a copy of the page,

00:13:36.261 --> 00:13:37.821 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and only on paper or demo.

00:13:39.021 --> 00:13:41.382 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring a notebook, and no secrets: no

00:13:41.382 --> 00:13:44.814 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
seed phrase, private key or API secret.

00:13:45.334 --> 00:13:47.443 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A seed phrase or private key is the

00:13:47.443 --> 00:13:49.627 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
master password to a crypto wallet.

00:13:50.147 --> 00:13:52.950 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An API secret is a key that lets an

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.899 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
app place trades on your account,

00:13:54.899 --> 00:13:56.921 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and sometimes move money out of it.

00:13:57.971 --> 00:13:59.663 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair, in the next lesson,

00:13:59.663 --> 00:14:01.278 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is the two things you swap,

00:14:01.278 --> 00:14:03.047 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
like bitcoin and US dollars.

00:14:04.097 --> 00:14:06.338 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buys below

00:14:06.338 --> 00:14:08.579 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the current price and waiting sells

00:14:08.579 --> 00:14:10.670 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above it, inside a range you choose.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:12.988 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A finished pair buys

00:14:12.988 --> 00:14:14.273 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower and sells higher.

00:14:14.479 --> 00:14:16.770 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:14:16.831 --> 00:14:18.889 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example that was about 8

00:14:18.889 --> 00:14:21.111 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
dollars 46, and it is not a promise.

00:14:22.311 --> 00:14:23.708 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the top and

00:14:23.708 --> 00:14:24.881 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you hold mostly cash.

00:14:25.331 --> 00:14:26.671 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the bottom

00:14:26.671 --> 00:14:28.267 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold mostly coin.

00:14:29.467 --> 00:14:31.979 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a daily close leaves the range and the

00:14:31.979 --> 00:14:34.491 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside, you stop the

00:14:34.491 --> 00:14:36.390 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:39.815 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Next lesson: What you are going to need.

00:14:40.204 --> 00:14:41.824 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring your notes: your range,

00:14:41.824 --> 00:14:43.444 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your gaps and your stop rule.

